; `BigInt(<String>)` — the only spelling that can express a value beyond Long range.
;
; Both fronts lower every `BigInt(x)` to `(prim i->big x)` whatever `x` is: they are untyped, so
; they cannot route a String to a different prim. The VM's `i->big` accepted an Int and nothing
; else, so on the native and bytecode lanes there was no way to write a big integer that is
; actually big — most of the point of the type. `bigint-from-a-string-is-unsupported-on-native-
; and-bytecode`, measured against the interpreter, which prints the number and `false`.
;
; Row 2 is why a construction row alone is not enough: it MULTIPLIES the parsed value, so a lane
; that quietly kept the string (or truncated it into a machine int) cannot answer it. Row 3 is the
; ANTI-ROW — the Int spelling that always worked must keep working, since the fix widens the very
; arm it goes through.
(program
 (defs
  (def main
   (lam 0
    (seq
     ; construction from a String, well past Long range
     (prim __autoOutput__
      (prim big->str (prim i->big (lit (str "123456789012345678901234567890")))))
     (seq
      ; ARITHMETIC on the parsed value — the row a string-shaped fake cannot answer
      (prim __autoOutput__
       (prim big->str
        (prim big.mul
         (prim i->big (lit (str "123456789012345678901234567890")))
         (prim i->big (lit (int 2))))))
      ; ANTI-ROW: the Int spelling still constructs the same way it always did
      (prim __autoOutput__
       (prim big->str (prim i->big (lit (int 7))))))))))
 (entry (app (global main))))
